4 THE R𝜔 CALCULUS
This section provides a formal description of the syntax and type system of R𝜔. As in Morris and McKinna
[2019], R𝜔 is parameterized by a row theory, giving the intended interpretation of rows. A row the-
ory T is a triple h⊢ T , ≡ T , T i, where
      • ⊢ T is a kinding relation, capturing when rows are well-formed;
      • ≡ T is an equivalence relation, identifying rows; and,
      • T is an entailment relation, giving the meaning of the . and ⊙ predicates.
We write R𝜔 (T ) to indicate R𝜔 instantiated with theory T . Our description of R𝜔 syntax (§4.1),
types (§4.2), and terms (§4.3) are all given generically over an arbitrary row theory T . We then
provide three concrete row theories. The minimal row theory (§4.4) captures labeled rows, but
makes no commitment to when (non-singleton) rows are well-formed. The examples in the pre-
vious section are all well-typed given only the minimal row theory. We then describe the simple
row theory (§4.5), which captures commutative Rémy-style rows, and the scoped row theory (§4.6),
which captures non-commutative Leijen-style rows. We develop the expected metatheory in the
following section, when we discuss our denotational interpretation of R𝜔 in Agda.

4.1      Syntax
The syntax of R𝜔 (T ) is given in Figure 8.
    Kinds include types ★, labels L, rows R𝜅 of kind 𝜅, and type constructors 𝜅 → 𝜅. Not all possible
kinds are currently used in R𝜔. For example: while nothing prevents describing a type of kind RL
(i.e., a row of labels), we have no primitives that operate on such a type, and indeed suspect that
such a type would be very diﬃcult to use (§7).
    Predicates include containment 𝜌 .d 𝜌 and combination 𝜌 ⊙𝜌 ∼ 𝜌. To account for non-commutative
row theories, we include directed variants of the containment predicate; intuitively, if 𝜌 1 ⊙ 𝜌 2 ∼ 𝜌 3 ,
then 𝜌 1 .L 𝜌 3 and 𝜌 2 .R 𝜌 3 . Given a commutative row theory, these predicates are equivalent. In
a practical language based on R𝜔, we anticipate that the predicate language would be extended
with other forms of predicates, such as type classes [Wadler and Blott 1989], linearity constraints
[Gan et al. 2014; Morris 2016], or general equality constraints [Peyton Jones et al. 2006].
    We let 𝜙, 𝜏, 𝜐, 𝜌 and 𝜉 range over types; when possible, we use 𝜙 where we expect a type con-
structor, 𝜌 where we expect a row type, and 𝜉 where we expect a label. Standard type constructs
include variables 𝛼, constants (here only the function arrow), quantiﬁers, abstractions, and appli-
cations. Predicates appear in qualiﬁed types 𝜋 ⇒ 𝜏. To incorporate labeling, we include labels (ℓ)
themselves, singletons ⌊𝜉⌋, and labeled types 𝜉 ⊲ 𝜏. Following Rose, we treat labeled types and row

